From: Lumia 1520 mainline port
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qdsp6: add a service-inventory diagnostic probe

Mainline's APR does not discover services: of_register_apr_devices() creates
one device per DT child with a "reg" and the driver binds whether or not the
firmware implements anything at that address. So "what audio services does
this ADSP have" cannot be read out of DT or sysfs -- it has to be asked.

q6core carries the query but cannot be used to ask it. q6core_probe() sends
no packets; the AVCS version request is lazy, behind q6core_get_svc_api_info(),
whose only in-tree callers are q6afe, q6asm and q6adm. And q6core never
reports the reply: it is kmemdup'd into a private struct behind a file-scope
static g_core, with no sysfs, no debugfs and no printk of the service table.

This adds a default-off developer fixture supplying the two missing halves: a
trigger that calls the exported q6core_get_svc_api_info(), and an observer --
a kprobe on q6core's private callback -- that captures the raw reply payload
before q6core consumes it. Nothing in q6core is modified.

There is exactly one usable query per boot: is_version_requested latches
unconditionally after the first attempt, and a 100 ms timeout in
q6core_get_fwk_versions() returns 0 rather than -ENOTSUPP, so the legacy
AVCS_GET_VERSIONS fallback is never transmitted after a silent first request.
The harness therefore enforces observer-registered -> ARM -> FIRE as a
one-way state machine with a single fire, so a query can never go out
unobserved and be misreported as a silent DSP.

Not upstream material: this is a bring-up instrument.

---

diff -uNr orig/sound/soc/qcom/Kconfig new/sound/soc/qcom/Kconfig
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/Kconfig	2026-08-17 16:56:35.046389785 -0400
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/Kconfig	2026-08-17 16:57:17.108081149 -0400
@@ -156,6 +156,27 @@
 	 audio drivers. This includes q6asm, q6adm,
 	 q6afe interfaces to DSP using apr.
 
+config SND_SOC_QDSP6_INVENTORY_PROBE
+	tristate "QDSP6 service-inventory diagnostic probe (developers only)"
+	depends on SND_SOC_QDSP6_CORE && KPROBES && DEBUG_FS
+	depends on ARM
+	default n
+	help
+	  Developer diagnostic fixture, not audio architecture.
+
+	  Asks the ADSP firmware which audio services it actually
+	  implements, by triggering q6core's lazy AVCS version query and
+	  observing the raw reply with a kprobe on q6core's private
+	  callback. q6core neither sends anything on probe nor reports the
+	  reply to userspace, so neither half is reachable without this.
+
+	  ARM-only: the observer reads the probed function's second
+	  argument out of the argument register directly, because
+	  regs_get_kernel_argument() is not defined for ARM.
+
+	  Say N unless you are bringing up a Q6 audio path and need to
+	  know what the firmware contains. If unsure, say N.
+
 config SND_SOC_MSM8996
 	tristate "SoC Machine driver for MSM8996 and APQ8096 boards"
 	depends on QCOM_APR
diff -uNr orig/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/Makefile new/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/Makefile
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/Makefile	2026-08-17 16:56:35.051922798 -0400
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/Makefile	2026-08-17 16:57:17.111412700 -0400
@@ -18,3 +18,6 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_SND_SOC_QDSP6_PRM) += q6prm.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_SND_SOC_QDSP6_PRM_LPASS_CLOCKS) += q6prm-clocks.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_SND_SOC_QDSP6_USB) += q6usb.o
+
+# Diagnostic fixture, default off. See SND_SOC_QDSP6_INVENTORY_PROBE.
+obj-$(CONFIG_SND_SOC_QDSP6_INVENTORY_PROBE) += q6inventory_probe.o
diff -uNr orig/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6inventory_probe.c new/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6inventory_probe.c
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6inventory_probe.c	1969-12-31 19:00:00.000000000 -0500
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6inventory_probe.c	2026-08-17 16:56:35.059203468 -0400
@@ -0,0 +1,599 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * QDSP6 service-inventory diagnostic probe.
+ *
+ * ONE QUESTION: what audio services does this ADSP firmware actually have?
+ *
+ * This is a developer diagnostic fixture, not audio architecture. It exists
+ * because stock q6core cannot answer that question:
+ *
+ *   - q6core_probe() sends no packets. It kzallocs, sets drvdata, inits a
+ *     mutex and a waitqueue, returns 0. Binding q6core puts nothing on the
+ *     wire, ever. The version query is lazy, behind q6core_get_svc_api_info(),
+ *     whose only in-tree callers are q6afe, q6asm and q6adm -- none of which
+ *     may be instantiated at this stage.
+ *
+ *   - q6core never reports the reply to anyone. It is kmemdup'd into a
+ *     private struct behind a file-scope static g_core, with no sysfs, no
+ *     debugfs and no printk of the service table.
+ *
+ * So the firmware's answer needs a TRIGGER (this module calls the exported
+ * API) and an OBSERVER (a kprobe on q6core's private callback, which sees the
+ * raw payload before q6core consumes it). Nothing in q6core is modified.
+ *
+ * THERE IS EXACTLY ONE USABLE QUERY PER BOOT
+ *
+ * q6core_get_svc_api_info() latches is_version_requested unconditionally after
+ * the first attempt -- success, refusal, or silence alike -- and there is no
+ * retry path. Worse, q6core_get_fwk_versions() returns wait_event_timeout()'s
+ * rc, which is 0 on timeout rather than -ENOTSUPP, so after a silent first
+ * request the legacy AVCS_GET_VERSIONS opcode is never transmitted at all.
+ *
+ * That single fact shapes everything here. A badly observed first trigger
+ * consumes the boot's only query state irrecoverably, so:
+ *
+ *   observer registered  ->  ARM permitted  ->  FIRE permitted
+ *
+ * is enforced in code, one-way, one fire. If the observer could not be
+ * registered, ARM fails and FIRE is impossible -- because the worst outcome
+ * this experiment can produce is a query that went out unobserved and got
+ * reported as "the DSP was silent". That looks exactly like a real firmware
+ * finding and is not one.
+ *
+ * The handler captures bytes; it does not interpret them. Parsing happens in
+ * userspace, so the firmware's actual payload survives a parser that later
+ * turns out to be wrong.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/device.h>
+#include <linux/fs.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/kprobes.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/spinlock.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
+#include <linux/uaccess.h>
+#include <linux/soc/qcom/apr.h>
+
+#include "q6core.h"
+
+/*
+ * The second argument to q6core_callback() -- struct apr_resp_pkt * -- under
+ * AAPCS. regs_get_kernel_argument() is not defined for ARM, so the register
+ * is read directly. Guarded rather than #ifdef'd silently: if this is ever
+ * built for another architecture the register mapping is wrong, not merely
+ * missing.
+ */
+#if defined(CONFIG_ARM)
+#define Q6INV_ARG1(regs)	((regs)->ARM_r1)
+#else
+#error "q6inventory_probe: argument-register access is ARM-specific"
+#endif
+
+#define AVCS_CMD_GET_FWK_VERSION	0x0001292c
+#define AVCS_CMDRSP_GET_FWK_VERSION	0x0001292d
+#define AVCS_GET_VERSIONS		0x00012905
+#define AVCS_GET_VERSIONS_RSP		0x00012906
+#define ADSP_EUNSUPPORTED		0x00000003
+
+#define Q6INV_PROBED_SYMBOL	"q6core_callback"
+#define Q6INV_Q6CORE_DRIVER	"qcom-q6core"
+
+/*
+ * Generous for an APR packet: even a firmware listing every service it could
+ * plausibly have is a few hundred bytes. Sized so that truncation should be
+ * impossible in practice -- and reported loudly if it happens anyway, because
+ * a partial capture must never be called "the service inventory".
+ */
+#define Q6INV_CAP		4096
+
+/* Deliberately awkward, so nothing fires by accident or by tab-completion. */
+#define Q6INV_TOKEN_ARM		"arm-q6-inventory"
+#define Q6INV_TOKEN_FIRE	"fire-q6-inventory"
+
+/* The services the conventional QDSP6 playback path needs, plus core. */
+static const int q6inv_svc_ids[] = { 3, 4, 7, 8 };
+#define Q6INV_NSVC	ARRAY_SIZE(q6inv_svc_ids)
+
+enum q6inv_state {
+	Q6INV_UNARMED = 0,
+	Q6INV_ARMED,
+	Q6INV_FIRED,
+};
+
+static const char * const q6inv_state_name[] = {
+	[Q6INV_UNARMED]	= "UNARMED",
+	[Q6INV_ARMED]	= "ARMED",
+	[Q6INV_FIRED]	= "FIRED",
+};
+
+/*
+ * Observer state. Written from the kprobe pre-handler, which runs with
+ * preemption disabled out of a breakpoint exception, so it is protected by an
+ * irqsave spinlock and never by the mutex.
+ */
+struct q6inv_capture {
+	spinlock_t		lock;
+
+	unsigned int		callback_hits;
+
+	bool			fwk_response_seen;
+	bool			legacy_response_seen;
+	bool			basic_rsp_seen;
+	u32			basic_rsp_original_opcode;
+	u32			basic_rsp_status;
+
+	unsigned int		unknown_opcode_hits;
+	u32			last_unknown_opcode;
+
+	bool			inventory_payload_seen;
+	u32			inventory_opcode;
+	int			payload_size;
+	int			captured_size;
+	bool			truncated;
+	u8			buf[Q6INV_CAP];
+};
+
+/*
+ * Trigger state. Touched only from debugfs write/read paths, which may sleep
+ * -- q6core_get_svc_api_info() takes a mutex and waits on a completion -- so
+ * it is protected by the mutex and never by the spinlock.
+ */
+struct q6inv_trigger {
+	struct mutex		lock;
+	enum q6inv_state	state;
+	unsigned int		fire_count;
+
+	int			svc_ret[Q6INV_NSVC];
+	struct q6core_svc_api_info svc_info[Q6INV_NSVC];
+	bool			svc_poison_intact[Q6INV_NSVC];
+};
+
+static struct q6inv_capture q6inv_cap;
+static struct q6inv_trigger q6inv_trg;
+
+static bool q6inv_probe_registered;
+static unsigned long q6inv_resolved_address;
+static struct dentry *q6inv_dir;
+
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------ observer -- */
+
+static void q6inv_capture_payload(struct q6inv_capture *c,
+				  u32 opcode, const void *payload, int size)
+{
+	c->inventory_payload_seen = true;
+	c->inventory_opcode = opcode;
+	c->payload_size = size;
+
+	if (!payload || size <= 0) {
+		c->captured_size = 0;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (size > Q6INV_CAP) {
+		/*
+		 * Recorded, not hidden. The evidence script refuses an A/B
+		 * verdict when this is set: a partial table cannot establish
+		 * that a service is absent, only that it was not in the part
+		 * we managed to keep.
+		 */
+		c->truncated = true;
+		c->captured_size = Q6INV_CAP;
+	} else {
+		c->captured_size = size;
+	}
+
+	memcpy(c->buf, payload, c->captured_size);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Deliberately boring. No allocation, no printk, no parsing of the variable
+ * table -- one memcpy and some scalars. Everything interpretive happens later,
+ * in userspace, from the bytes kept here.
+ */
+static int q6inv_pre_handler(struct kprobe *p, struct pt_regs *regs)
+{
+	struct q6inv_capture *c = &q6inv_cap;
+	struct apr_resp_pkt *data;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	u32 opcode;
+
+	data = (struct apr_resp_pkt *)Q6INV_ARG1(regs);
+	if (!data)
+		return 0;
+
+	opcode = data->hdr.opcode;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&c->lock, flags);
+
+	c->callback_hits++;
+
+	switch (opcode) {
+	case AVCS_CMDRSP_GET_FWK_VERSION:
+		c->fwk_response_seen = true;
+		q6inv_capture_payload(c, opcode, data->payload,
+				      data->payload_size);
+		break;
+
+	case AVCS_GET_VERSIONS_RSP:
+		c->legacy_response_seen = true;
+		q6inv_capture_payload(c, opcode, data->payload,
+				      data->payload_size);
+		break;
+
+	case APR_BASIC_RSP_RESULT:
+		/*
+		 * The embedded opcode is the request being answered and status
+		 * is the ADSP's verdict on it. status == ADSP_EUNSUPPORTED
+		 * against 0x0001292c is the one thing that mechanically proves
+		 * the legacy fallback was LICENSED rather than skipped -- and
+		 * it is what stops an unsupported-command response being
+		 * misread as an inventory.
+		 */
+		c->basic_rsp_seen = true;
+		if (data->payload &&
+		    data->payload_size >= (int)sizeof(struct aprv2_ibasic_rsp_result_t)) {
+			const struct aprv2_ibasic_rsp_result_t *r = data->payload;
+
+			c->basic_rsp_original_opcode = r->opcode;
+			c->basic_rsp_status = r->status;
+		}
+		break;
+
+	default:
+		c->unknown_opcode_hits++;
+		c->last_unknown_opcode = opcode;
+		break;
+	}
+
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&c->lock, flags);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static struct kprobe q6inv_kp = {
+	.symbol_name	= Q6INV_PROBED_SYMBOL,
+	.pre_handler	= q6inv_pre_handler,
+};
+
+/* ----------------------------------------------------------- prerequisite -- */
+
+static int q6inv_is_q6core(struct device *dev, void *data)
+{
+	return dev->driver && !strcmp(dev->driver->name, Q6INV_Q6CORE_DRIVER);
+}
+
+/*
+ * q6core_get_svc_api_info() opens with
+ *
+ *	if (!g_core || !ainfo)
+ *		return 0;
+ *
+ * so calling it without an instantiated q6core returns "success" having
+ * written nothing. This check makes that branch unreachable; the poison in
+ * q6inv_fire() makes it detectable if it somehow happens anyway.
+ */
+static bool q6inv_q6core_bound(void)
+{
+	return bus_for_each_dev(&aprbus, NULL, NULL, q6inv_is_q6core) != 0;
+}
+
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------- trigger -- */
+
+static void q6inv_fire(struct q6inv_trigger *t)
+{
+	size_t i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < Q6INV_NSVC; i++) {
+		struct q6core_svc_api_info ai;
+
+		/*
+		 * Poison before every call. Note which fields: the helper
+		 * writes api_version and api_branch_version but NEVER
+		 * service_id, so service_id keeps its poison even on a hit and
+		 * must be excluded from the test.
+		 */
+		memset(&ai, 0xff, sizeof(ai));
+
+		t->svc_ret[i] = q6core_get_svc_api_info(q6inv_svc_ids[i], &ai);
+		t->svc_info[i] = ai;
+		t->svc_poison_intact[i] = (ai.api_version == 0xffffffffu &&
+					   ai.api_branch_version == 0xffffffffu);
+	}
+
+	t->fire_count++;
+
+	/*
+	 * Only the FIRST call performed a transaction; the rest were lookups
+	 * into whatever it cached. They are a cross-check of the helper
+	 * against the observer's captured table, not four independent probes.
+	 */
+}
+
+/* -------------------------------------------------------------- debugfs -- */
+
+static int q6inv_read_token(const char __user *ubuf, size_t len,
+			    char *out, size_t outsz)
+{
+	size_t n = min(len, outsz - 1);
+
+	if (copy_from_user(out, ubuf, n))
+		return -EFAULT;
+
+	out[n] = '\0';
+	strim(out);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static ssize_t q6inv_arm_write(struct file *f, const char __user *ubuf,
+			       size_t len, loff_t *off)
+{
+	struct q6inv_trigger *t = &q6inv_trg;
+	char tok[32];
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = q6inv_read_token(ubuf, len, tok, sizeof(tok));
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	if (strcmp(tok, Q6INV_TOKEN_ARM))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
+	 * The observer gates ARM, not FIRE. Gating only FIRE would permit an
+	 * armed-but-unobserved state to exist at all, and that state is the
+	 * one that manufactures a false C2.
+	 */
+	if (!q6inv_probe_registered)
+		return -ENXIO;
+	if (!q6inv_q6core_bound())
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	mutex_lock(&t->lock);
+	if (t->state != Q6INV_UNARMED) {
+		mutex_unlock(&t->lock);
+		return -EALREADY;
+	}
+	t->state = Q6INV_ARMED;
+	mutex_unlock(&t->lock);
+
+	return len;
+}
+
+static ssize_t q6inv_fire_write(struct file *f, const char __user *ubuf,
+				size_t len, loff_t *off)
+{
+	struct q6inv_trigger *t = &q6inv_trg;
+	char tok[32];
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = q6inv_read_token(ubuf, len, tok, sizeof(tok));
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	if (strcmp(tok, Q6INV_TOKEN_FIRE))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	mutex_lock(&t->lock);
+
+	if (t->state == Q6INV_UNARMED) {
+		mutex_unlock(&t->lock);
+		return -EPERM;
+	}
+	/*
+	 * No re-arm, no second fire, no path back. After a silent first
+	 * request q6core has latched is_version_requested and there is nothing
+	 * left to retry this boot; allowing a second attempt would only let it
+	 * be mistaken for the first.
+	 */
+	if (t->state == Q6INV_FIRED) {
+		mutex_unlock(&t->lock);
+		return -EALREADY;
+	}
+
+	t->state = Q6INV_FIRED;
+	q6inv_fire(t);
+
+	mutex_unlock(&t->lock);
+	return len;
+}
+
+/* no_llseek was removed in 6.12; a NULL .llseek is the modern spelling. */
+static const struct file_operations q6inv_arm_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.open	= simple_open,
+	.write	= q6inv_arm_write,
+};
+
+static const struct file_operations q6inv_fire_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.open	= simple_open,
+	.write	= q6inv_fire_write,
+};
+
+static ssize_t q6inv_status_read(struct file *f, char __user *ubuf,
+				 size_t len, loff_t *off)
+{
+	struct q6inv_capture *c = &q6inv_cap;
+	struct q6inv_trigger *t = &q6inv_trg;
+	struct q6inv_capture snap;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	size_t i, n = 0;
+	char *buf;
+	ssize_t ret;
+
+	buf = kzalloc(PAGE_SIZE, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	/* Snapshot the scalars without the payload; the buffer is large. */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&c->lock, flags);
+	snap = *c;
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&c->lock, flags);
+
+	mutex_lock(&t->lock);
+
+	n += scnprintf(buf + n, PAGE_SIZE - n,
+		       "state=%s\n"
+		       "probe_registered=%d\n"
+		       "probed_symbol=%s\n"
+		       "resolved_address=0x%08lx\n"
+		       "q6core_bound=%d\n"
+		       "fire_count=%u\n"
+		       "callback_hits=%u\n"
+		       "first_request_opcode=0x%08x\n",
+		       q6inv_state_name[t->state],
+		       q6inv_probe_registered ? 1 : 0,
+		       Q6INV_PROBED_SYMBOL,
+		       q6inv_resolved_address,
+		       q6inv_q6core_bound() ? 1 : 0,
+		       t->fire_count,
+		       snap.callback_hits,
+		       AVCS_CMD_GET_FWK_VERSION);
+
+	n += scnprintf(buf + n, PAGE_SIZE - n,
+		       "fwk_response_seen=%d\n"
+		       "basic_rsp_seen=%d\n"
+		       "basic_rsp_original_opcode=0x%08x\n"
+		       "basic_rsp_status=0x%08x\n"
+		       "basic_rsp_is_unsupported=%d\n"
+		       "legacy_response_seen=%d\n"
+		       "legacy_response_opcode=0x%08x\n"
+		       "unknown_opcode_hits=%u\n"
+		       "last_unknown_opcode=0x%08x\n",
+		       snap.fwk_response_seen ? 1 : 0,
+		       snap.basic_rsp_seen ? 1 : 0,
+		       snap.basic_rsp_original_opcode,
+		       snap.basic_rsp_status,
+		       (snap.basic_rsp_seen &&
+			snap.basic_rsp_status == ADSP_EUNSUPPORTED) ? 1 : 0,
+		       snap.legacy_response_seen ? 1 : 0,
+		       AVCS_GET_VERSIONS_RSP,
+		       snap.unknown_opcode_hits,
+		       snap.last_unknown_opcode);
+
+	n += scnprintf(buf + n, PAGE_SIZE - n,
+		       "inventory_payload_seen=%d\n"
+		       "inventory_opcode=0x%08x\n"
+		       "payload_size=%d\n"
+		       "captured_size=%d\n"
+		       "capture_capacity=%d\n"
+		       "truncated=%d\n",
+		       snap.inventory_payload_seen ? 1 : 0,
+		       snap.inventory_opcode,
+		       snap.payload_size,
+		       snap.captured_size,
+		       Q6INV_CAP,
+		       snap.truncated ? 1 : 0);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < Q6INV_NSVC; i++)
+		n += scnprintf(buf + n, PAGE_SIZE - n,
+			       "svc%d_ret=%d svc%d_api_version=0x%08x "
+			       "svc%d_api_branch=0x%08x svc%d_poison_intact=%d\n",
+			       q6inv_svc_ids[i], t->svc_ret[i],
+			       q6inv_svc_ids[i], t->svc_info[i].api_version,
+			       q6inv_svc_ids[i], t->svc_info[i].api_branch_version,
+			       q6inv_svc_ids[i], t->svc_poison_intact[i] ? 1 : 0);
+
+	mutex_unlock(&t->lock);
+
+	ret = simple_read_from_buffer(ubuf, len, off, buf, n);
+	kfree(buf);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations q6inv_status_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.open	= simple_open,
+	.read	= q6inv_status_read,
+	.llseek	= default_llseek,
+};
+
+/*
+ * The firmware's own bytes, verbatim. Nothing here interprets them: if the
+ * userspace parser is later found wrong, this survives to be re-read.
+ */
+static ssize_t q6inv_raw_read(struct file *f, char __user *ubuf,
+			      size_t len, loff_t *off)
+{
+	struct q6inv_capture *c = &q6inv_cap;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int captured;
+	void *tmp;
+	ssize_t ret;
+
+	tmp = kzalloc(Q6INV_CAP, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!tmp)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&c->lock, flags);
+	captured = c->captured_size;
+	if (captured > 0)
+		memcpy(tmp, c->buf, captured);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&c->lock, flags);
+
+	ret = simple_read_from_buffer(ubuf, len, off, tmp, max(captured, 0));
+	kfree(tmp);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations q6inv_raw_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.open	= simple_open,
+	.read	= q6inv_raw_read,
+	.llseek	= default_llseek,
+};
+
+/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- init -- */
+
+static int __init q6inv_init(void)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	spin_lock_init(&q6inv_cap.lock);
+	mutex_init(&q6inv_trg.lock);
+
+	/*
+	 * Register the observer FIRST. If this fails the module still loads so
+	 * that status can report why, but probe_registered stays false and ARM
+	 * refuses -- a run that cannot observe must not be able to ask.
+	 */
+	ret = register_kprobe(&q6inv_kp);
+	if (ret) {
+		pr_err("q6inventory: register_kprobe(%s) failed: %d -- ARM will refuse\n",
+		       Q6INV_PROBED_SYMBOL, ret);
+	} else {
+		q6inv_probe_registered = true;
+		q6inv_resolved_address = (unsigned long)q6inv_kp.addr;
+		pr_info("q6inventory: observer on %s at 0x%08lx\n",
+			Q6INV_PROBED_SYMBOL, q6inv_resolved_address);
+	}
+
+	q6inv_dir = debugfs_create_dir("q6_inventory", NULL);
+	debugfs_create_file("arm", 0200, q6inv_dir, NULL, &q6inv_arm_fops);
+	debugfs_create_file("fire", 0200, q6inv_dir, NULL, &q6inv_fire_fops);
+	debugfs_create_file("status", 0400, q6inv_dir, NULL, &q6inv_status_fops);
+	debugfs_create_file("inventory_raw", 0400, q6inv_dir, NULL,
+			    &q6inv_raw_fops);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void __exit q6inv_exit(void)
+{
+	debugfs_remove_recursive(q6inv_dir);
+
+	/*
+	 * Safe by construction: this module references q6core_get_svc_api_info(),
+	 * so it holds a module dependency on q6core. q6core cannot be unloaded
+	 * while we are loaded, and the probe can never point into freed text.
+	 */
+	if (q6inv_probe_registered)
+		unregister_kprobe(&q6inv_kp);
+}
+
+module_init(q6inv_init);
+module_exit(q6inv_exit);
+
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("QDSP6 service-inventory diagnostic probe");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL v2");
